abstract: >-
  Dobrushin's uniqueness criterion in its Wasserstein (W1) form, applied to the SU(2) Wilson lattice gauge
  measure on Z^4. With the Euclidean (chordal) metric of R^4 on SU(2) = S^3 (Levin's metric), the one-site
  coefficient in a field of strength kappa <= 4/3 equals E_kappa[x_perp^2] <= 1/4 - 0.0084 kappa^2, maximal at
  zero field; each of the 18 neighbouring links has influence exactly beta/4, the Dobrushin sum is 9 beta/2, and
  the Gibbs measure is unique for beta < 2/9 (weight exp(-beta sum_p (1 - Re Tr U_p / 2))). This confirms, for
  N = 4 and |g| <= 4/3, the maximum-at-zero-field property conjectured by Levin (1980, Remark 3.7), proved for N = 4
  and all fields by Bhattacharjee and Bhattacharya (2026, Theorem 7). With the
  geodesic metric the exact coefficient is 8 beta/(9 pi), giving the weaker range beta < pi/16. The one-link
  inequalities are proved by computer-assisted arguments (explicit fields, Arb interval arithmetic, independent
  recertification), and the final rational inequalities are checked in Lean 4 (axioms propext,
  Classical.choice, Quot.sound only). Uniqueness only: no decay of correlations, spectral gap or log-Sobolev
  inequality is claimed. Prior public statement: the uniqueness range beta < 2/9 for the four-dimensional SU(2) model (with exponential decay and a lattice mass gap) and the all-field bound W1(nu_b, nu_b') <= |b - b'|/4 on S^3 were stated publicly, with proofs, in the preprint of D. Bhattacharjee and U. Bhattacharya, "Mass Gap of Lattice Yang-Mills Theory up to the Dobrushin Ceiling", hexagon:2610.00341v1, 18 October 2026 (Theorems E, F and 7). This note was prepared independently and makes no priority claim. Its own contributions: the exact one-link chordal coefficient E_kappa[x_perp^2] for kappa <= 4/3 with an explicit remainder, the exact geodesic coefficient 8 beta/(9 pi), two independent interval-arithmetic certificates, Lean-checked final steps, and explicit transfers of the published constants of Levin, Royer, Gross, Simon and Shen-Zhu-Zhu to the Wilson link.
